⚠ THE ONE JUDGEMENT THAT WAS OPEN IS ANSWERED, AND THE OBVIOUS ANSWER WAS WRONG

The BEST GUESS this file carried said: settle whether `term.tag` is always the
right slot, and the candidate becomes a remedy. Settled, by reading the two
places that mint a `slot` attribute. There are exactly two, and they disagree:

    planTerm   at >= 1   slot.text = term.tag      a TERM position
    planRule   at == 0   slot.text = rule.tag      THE ZERO-MEANS-SELF MARKER,
                         literal = rule.text        and there is NO term at all

`break` -- this file's own subject -- plans at 0. It is a rule with no terms
whose match content is its own tag, picked up by bear-trap 26's fallback, which
planRule documents as doing real work rather than as an accident. So a
one-argument litToK that resolved `gKantRule->get(n)` unconditionally would have
refused THE SUBJECT while passing on every term-position specimen -- green on
Braced and Parens, red on break, with nothing naming why.

⚠ IT STILL TAKES ONE ARGUMENT. Both slots are derivable from the index once the
FRAME is consulted, and the frame already holds the rule as gKantRule. So litK's
stated law survives intact -- A KANT BODY NAMES A TERM AND NOTHING ELSE -- and
the two-argument shape this file listed as the killing outcome is not needed.
Zero-means-self is the landed convention of 2026-08-24 (rule-ladder rung two);
